<Base title="Holiday scams: fake delivery texts, fake shops and gift card tricks" description="The scams that peak from November to January, how to spot them, and the one right move for each.">
  <div class="page">
    <h1>Holiday scams: delivery texts, fake shops and gift cards</h1>
    <p class="lead">More parcels, more shopping and more gifts means more scams. Here are the three that keep coming back, and what to do about each.</p>

    <h2>1. The “missed delivery” text</h2>
    <p>A text or email says your parcel is held, the address is wrong, or there’s a small fee to redeliver it. The link goes to a fake site that takes your card details. These come all year, but there are more when you’re expecting gifts.</p>
    <div class="move">
      <strong>The right move</strong>
      Don’t tap the link. Track the parcel yourself in the shop’s app, or on the carrier’s own website you type in.
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>US: USPS won’t contact you out of the blue about a delivery unless you asked for updates, and never asks you to pay to redeliver.</li>
      <li>UK: Royal Mail asks for unpaid postage by leaving a grey Fee to Pay card. It doesn’t collect redelivery fees by text.</li>
      <li>Forward the text to 7726 in the US and UK, then delete it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. The fake shop with amazing prices</h2>
    <p>An ad on social media shows popular gifts at prices far below anywhere else. The site takes your money, then sends nothing, or something cheap and different.</p>
    <div class="move">
      <strong>The right move</strong>
      Look the shop up before you pay, and pay by credit card. <a href="/check-a-website/">Use our 5-step website check</a>.
    </div>

    <h2>3. “Pay with gift cards”</h2>
    <p>A seller, a “boss”, a “grandchild” or a “government office” asks you to pay with gift cards and read out the numbers on the back. Once they have those numbers, the money is gone.</p>
    <div class="move">
      <strong>The right move</strong>
      Gift cards are for gifts. Anyone who asks to be paid with them is a scammer. <a href="/how-to-pay-safely/">See which payments are safe</a>.
    </div>

    <h2>Also watch for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Fake charities</strong> that copy real names. Give through the charity’s own website, not a link in a message.</li>
      <li><strong>Sellers who only take payment apps, wire transfers or crypto.</strong> Only scammers tell you to pay that way.</li>
      <li><strong>Travel deals</strong> for holiday trips that need full payment today by bank transfer.</li>
    </ul>

